# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

# Reverse-path-forwarding check — a PROCEDURE that reads RIGHT TO LEFT.
#
# GENRE: `flowchart`, and it was `block` until this release. What the figure IS
# never changed — a chain of stages, one decision, two terminal outcomes — but
# under `block` the roles were carried by `shape=`: a diamond MEANT "decision"
# and a rounded box MEANT "outcome", to a reader who had learned that. Shape is
# presentation (`MEANING-RECOVERY-SOURCE`) and a convention has to be learned, while a word is read,
# so those facts were asserted by nothing a reading agent could recover. Under
# `flowchart` they are the keywords `process`, `decision` and `terminator`, the
# geometry DERIVES from the role (box, diamond, rounded), and the figure states
# what each stage is instead of drawing a hint at it (`DOMAIN-CONVENTION-DIRECTIVES`).
#
# THE PRICE, STATED: genre `flowchart` is EXPERIMENTAL — outside the
# v0.1 conformance surface, so it may change or be withdrawn in a later `0.x`,
# where `block` is normative. The declared version does NOT move: `flowchart`
# has been dispatchable under `figdown 0.1` since the genre existed, and `edge`
# is its connector word at this version (`flowline` is the 0.2 spelling and a
# line error here). The four demonstrators below are genre-independent and all
# four still execute — which is the only reason the trade was payable.
#
# What this figure probes: four constructs that had no demonstrator anywhere in
# the corpus before 0.1. They are gathered into one small document on
# purpose — a capability needs A demonstrator, not a document of its own.
#
#   1. `flow left`   — the reverse direction. Twelve figures in this corpus use
#                      `flow right` and twelve use `flow down`; `left` and `up`
#                      had none, so half of the enum was untested by any figure.
#   2. `\"` escape   — a literal double quote INSIDE a quoted label.
#   3. `#` inside a  — a `[...]` region is verbatim (`VERBATIM-REGION-SCOPE`): the
#      bracketed        comment mark loses its meaning inside one, so an edge
#      edge label       label may carry a `#` without quoting or escaping it.
#   4. a NEGATIVE   — `at=` is canvas px and the origin is not a wall. A pin may
#      `at=` pin        sit at a negative coordinate; the canvas still starts at
#                       (0,0) because it grows right and down only, so what the
#                       negative value spends is the margin already above the
#                       content — and no more than that. See the pin's own note.
#
# The subject fits the direction: an RPF check runs BACKWARDS along the path a
# packet arrived on, so a figure that reads right to left is the honest layout,
# not a stunt. That is the test for `flow left` in real use — if reversing the
# reading order does not say something about the subject, use `flow right`.
figdown 0.1 flowchart
title "RPF check on a multicast join"

class fail "RPF failure — the packet arrived on an interface that is not the route back to the source" stroke=#b91c1c style=dashed

# The source is on the RIGHT because the check walks back TOWARDS it. It is an
# `external`, not a `terminator`: the packet's origin is outside this procedure
# and this figure does not describe it, so the edge simply ends open at a label.
external src "from 198.51.100.7"
# `\n` (a line break) and `\\` (a literal backslash) are the other two escapes
# the grammar defines — core §11 lists exactly three, and all three are in this
# file. The interface filter is a regex, which is where a backslash occurs
# naturally in a network figure rather than as a demonstration of itself.
#
# NO `shape=` KEY SURVIVES THE CONVERSION, on purpose: the role supplies the
# geometry, and re-stating it would put the drawing back in the author's hands
# where the word already decides it. One visible delta and it is the honest one
# — the ingress stage used to be drawn `shape=rounded` and now draws as a plain
# box, because that is what `process` derives. The rounded corner was never
# meaning; `terminator` (accept, drop) is where rounded means "this is where the
# procedure ends", and those two still draw rounded because their ROLE says so.
process rx "Ingress ge-0/0/1\nfilter ge-0/0/\\d+"
process lookup "Unicast RIB lookup"
# The escape `\"` puts a literal double quote inside a quoted label. The label
# is one token to the parser either way — the quotes delimit it, the backslash
# says "this quote is data".
decision verdict "Arrived on the \"upstream\" interface?"
terminator accept "Accept and forward"
terminator drop "Drop (RPF fail)" class=fail

# `#` inside a bracketed edge label. Before `VERBATIM-REGION-SCOPE` this needed quoting, because
# the comment scanner ran before the label scanner and ate the rest of the line;
# the ruling made a verbatim region protect EVERY mark, not just the one that
# earned the rule. So `[VRF #7]` is six characters of label, not a comment.
edge src -[VRF #7]-> rx
edge rx -> lookup
edge lookup -[next-hop ge-0/0/1]-> verdict
edge verdict -[yes]-> accept
edge verdict -[no]-> drop class=fail

# NO layout zone, on purpose (maintainer review). This figure
# spent its whole life hand-tuned — `flow left`, a `rank`, and the corpus's
# only negative-coordinate `pin` — and the maintainer compared the tuned
# drawing against the genre's own default and ruled the default better: a
# top-down flowchart with the verdict fanning to its two outcomes is the
# drawing every reader already knows how to read. So the tuning went, which
# is itself the demonstration `GUI-WRITEBACK-STRUCTURE` makes: strip the layout zone and the figure
# says the same thing — here the stripped form is not merely equivalent but
# preferred. The negative-coordinate findings that used to live beside the
# pin (what `at=` does and does not do to the canvas) moved to
# examples/patterns/block-c.fd, whose side-cache lift is a subject that
# actually wants a negative coordinate, instead of riding a figure that no
# longer needs one.
